<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  EtherNet/IP for the `oil-gas-pipeline` persona (step H11). Bound on 44818,
  TCP and UDP.

  Persona rationale. EtherNet/IP on a Siemens PLC would be wrong, and
  Decision 18 asks for the protocol anyway because attacker tooling scans
  44818 regardless of what the device turns out to be and SIR-006-05 counts
  protocol reach. The resolution is the same one the substation persona uses:
  make it a SEPARATE device. A 1734-AENTR is a plain POINT I/O EtherNet/IP
  adapter, the kind of single-purpose third-party box that carries auxiliary
  station I/O — ESD loop monitoring, skid temperatures — on a brownfield
  compressor station. It is not a controller and it does not contradict the
  Siemens story.

  Mode. A real device answers explicit messaging on 44818/tcp AND ListIdentity
  discovery on 44818/udp, and the UDP half is what a scanner sends first
  (nmap's enip-info). Serving only TCP is itself a tell.

  Port 2222/udp is deliberately NOT served. It carries CIP class-1 implicit
  I/O, which Conpot cannot emulate at all; an open port that completes nothing
  is a stronger negative signal than a closed one.

  OPSEC. EnipServer.apply_identity feeds <device_info> to cpppo, so these
  fields are load-bearing rather than decorative: a template that leaves them
  at cpppo's built-in Rockwell 1756-L61/B LOGIX5561 (vendor 1, product code
  0x36, revision 0x0B14, serial 0x006C061A, status 0x3160) advertises a
  Conpot. Nothing here repeats one, and nothing repeats the substation
  persona's values either — two sensors answering with the same serial would
  correlate the fleet in one scan.

  StatusWord 0x0074 reads as configured (bit 2) with at least one connection
  established but none in run mode (extended status 7) — an I/O adapter that
  is online and owned, which is what an adapter looks like when the controller
  that owns it is idling.

  These are plausible values for the product, not values captured from a real
  1734-AENTR: the product code, revision, serial and status word are ours.
-->
<enip enabled="True" host="0.0.0.0" port="44818">
    <device_info>
        <VendorId>1</VendorId>
        <ProductName>1734-AENTR/C</ProductName>
        <!-- CIP device type 0x0C, Communications Adapter. -->
        <DeviceType>12</DeviceType>
        <SerialNumber>47105562</SerialNumber>
        <!-- major<<8 | minor -> 0x050E, revision 5.14 -->
        <ProductRevision>1294</ProductRevision>
        <ProductCode>168</ProductCode>
        <StatusWord>0x0074</StatusWord>
    </device_info>

    <mode>tcp,udp</mode>
    <latency>0.1</latency>
    <timeout>20</timeout>

    <!-- Auxiliary station I/O the adapter carries. Addresses are in the
         vendor-specific class range (0x64+) rather than upstream Conpot's
         @22/1/x, which is another default worth not repeating. -->
    <tags>
        <tag name="STN_ESD_LOOP_OK">
            <type>SINT</type>
            <size>1</size>
            <value>1</value>
            <addr>100/1/1</addr>
        </tag>
        <tag name="AUX_SKID_TEMP">
            <type>REAL</type>
            <size>4</size>
            <value>31.2</value>
            <addr>100/1/2</addr>
        </tag>
        <tag name="IO_RACK_ID">
            <type>SSTRING</type>
            <size>16</size>
            <value>CS-07-RACK-B</value>
            <addr>100/1/3</addr>
        </tag>
    </tags>
</enip>
